 of what  the parties mean and what are the sides  you know i've said for a long time wyatt  that i don't really find many trump  supporters to really be  conservative  uh  individuals in in the style of someone  like a mitt romney or even george bush i  consider them neo-reactionaries who  entered the political world because of  trump and to support trump and i don't  know where they're going to land at the  end of the day i don't know where  someone like joe manchin's going to land  at the end of the day  and obviously mitt romney for example as  you mentioned you brought up his name so  perhaps i'll go there he's a you know  he's a republican from a very  conservative state as opposed to someone  like joe manchin is a democrat from west  virginia which has been known as you  know a conservative state at least for  the past couple of election cycles so i  mean in a way you could you know say  that you know joe manchin may you know  have to be carefully looking at you know  um the policy that he kind of develops i  guess in order to  i guess represent the interests of you  know the people of west virginia how old  how big of a role i guess do you see um  you know the constituency area that you  represent playing in i guess in this  case as i mentioned joe manchin and the  views that he now holds  there's no set rule so if you are um a  left-leaning democrat in a very blue  state  you can more or less do whatever you  want because it's very unlikely that the  voters in that state are even going to  primary you never mind a vote for a  republican now you could say that that's  bad that it's too safe it's too comfy  for some of these members of the house  or senate that  they there's very little accountability  because it's sort of like a rubber stamp  or you could say that it gives them the  freedom to kind of be who they are you  know sometimes susan collins a senator  from maine or republican has mentioned  where there's the idea that because the  main the this is 